/-
# Automorphisms fixing only the identity conjugacy class (Kourovka Notebook Problem 3.51)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 3.51 (A. I. Saksonov): is it true that every
finite group `G` with a group of automorphisms `Φ` which acts regularly on the set of conjugacy
classes of `G` (that is, leaves only the identity class fixed) is soluble? The answer is known to be
affirmative when `Φ` is a cyclic group generated by a regular automorphism.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Solvable

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.AutomorphismsFixingOnlyIdentityClassKourovka3Problem51Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 3.51 (A. I.
Saksonov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700200 (`KOU-3.51`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`Φ` is a subgroup of `MulAut G`. "Leaves only the identity class fixed" means that no conjugacy
class of a non-identity element is mapped onto itself by every element of `Φ`: for each `g ≠ 1` some
`φ ∈ Φ` maps `g` outside its class (`¬ IsConj (φ g) g`). This is the reading of Fine, who restates
the problem as: must a finite group with no characteristic conjugacy class of non-trivial elements
be soluble? (Taking `Φ = Aut(G)` gives the weakest hypothesis, so the two forms are equivalent.)
-/

/-- The group `Φ` of automorphisms of `G` leaves only the identity conjugacy class fixed: every
conjugacy class of a non-identity element `g` is moved by some `φ ∈ Φ`, that is, `φ(g)` is not
conjugate to `g`. -/
def FixesOnlyIdentityClass {G : Type*} [Group G] (Φ : Subgroup (MulAut G)) : Prop :=
  ∀ g : G, g ≠ 1 → ∃ φ ∈ Φ, ¬ IsConj (φ g) g

/-- Kourovka 3.51 (A. I. Saksonov): every finite group `G` with a group `Φ` of automorphisms
leaving only the identity conjugacy class fixed is soluble. -/
def solubleIfAutomorphismsFixOnlyIdentityClass : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] (Φ : Subgroup (MulAut G)),
    FixesOnlyIdentityClass Φ → Group.IsSolvable G

/--
Resolved false: Fine (Theorem 2) constructs, for every finite group `A`, a finite group `G = H ⋊ A`
with `H` solvable and no characteristic conjugacy class of non-trivial cyclic subgroups; for
non-solvable `A` (e.g. `A₅`) the group `G` is non-soluble and `Φ = Aut(G)` fixes no non-identity
conjugacy class. The Kourovka Archive answers 3.51 "No, not always". Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D.
Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue,
arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 3.51 (A. I. Saksonov); resolution: Y.
Fine, Characteristic classes of involutions in nonsolvable groups, J. Group Theory 22 (2019), no. 6,
1077-1087, Section 1, Question 1 (Kourovka 3.51 in the form: must a finite group with no
characteristic conjugacy class of non-trivial elements be solvable?) and Theorem 2: for every finite
group `A` there is a finite group `G = H ⋊ A`, `H` solvable, with no characteristic conjugacy class
of non-trivial cyclic subgroups; the paper states that Theorem 2 answers Question 1 in the negative;
Kourovka Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 3.51: No, not always,
https://doi.org/10.1515/jgth-2019-0040.
-/
public theorem_wanted solubleIfAutomorphismsFixOnlyIdentityClass_refuted :
    ¬ solubleIfAutomorphismsFixOnlyIdentityClass

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.AutomorphismsFixingOnlyIdentityClassKourovka3Problem51Wanted
